{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>For honor and for splendor.</b> The reason the verse says this a second time is to allude to what they said in Zevachim (Zevachim 18b), and this is their language: if [the priestly garments] were faded or torn and he performed the service — his service is invalid. And it is further taught there: ״בַּד״ — that they must be new. And they said in the Talmud: new, yes; worn out, no; and the teaching of the Tanya: worn smooth are valid, etc., but it is a mitzvah, etc. Thus His saying <b>for honor</b> corresponds to torn garments, and this is a disqualification (le'ikkuvah), <b>and for splendor</b> corresponds to new garments, and this is a mitzvah. And for this reason the verse needed to state both of them; otherwise it should have said only ״for splendor,״ which is more than His saying ״for honor.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:18.187945958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}